        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Task Graph Analysis Flow\n├── Overseer Scorer\n│   └── scorer.Recommend() \u003c-- internal/analysis/overseer/scorer.go:65\n│       └── analyzer.Analyze(pending, wsID) \u003c-- 1a\n│           └── tasksgraph.Analyzer.Analyze() \u003c-- internal/analysis/tasksgraph/graph.go:50\n│               ├── Build directed graph\n│               │   ├── g = NewDirectedGraph() \u003c-- 1b\n│               │   ├── Add all task nodes \u003c-- internal/analysis/tasksgraph/graph.go:79\n│               │   └── Add dependency edges \u003c-- 1c\n│               ├── Compute graph metrics\n│               │   ├── PageRank(g, 0.85, 1e-6) \u003c-- 1d\n│               │   ├── computeCriticalPaths() \u003c-- 1e\n│               │   ├── Calculate in/out degrees \u003c-- internal/analysis/tasksgraph/graph.go:107\n│               │   └── topo.Sort(g) \u003c-- 1f\n│               └── return insights \u003c-- 1g\n└── Result: NodeMetrics with scores",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nIn a multi-agent task management system, **determining which task to work on next** is non-trivial. Tasks have dependencies (Task A blocks Task B), varying importance (some are on critical paths), and receive attention from human admins or system overseers through messages. The graph analysis system solves this by computing **quantitative metrics** that reveal which tasks are most blocking, most central to the dependency network, and most urgent based on human/system input.\n\nWithout this analysis, agents would need to manually traverse dependency chains, guess at task importance, or miss critical messages—leading to inefficient work ordering and blocked workflows.\n\n## Details\n\n### Graph Construction\n\nThe system models tasks as a **directed graph** where an edge from Task A to Task B means \"A depends on B\" [1b, 1c]. This representation enables algorithmic analysis of the dependency structure. Tasks become nodes, and `DependsOn` relationships become edges pointing toward dependencies.\n\n### Core Metrics\n\nThree key metrics are computed [1d, 1e]:\n\n1. **PageRank** (0.85 damping factor): Measures task centrality—tasks that many others depend on score higher. Uses the gonum network library's PageRank implementation.\n\n2. **Critical Path Score**: The longest path from a task to any sink (leaf task with no dependencies). Higher scores indicate tasks that block more downstream work. Computed via memoized depth-first search with cycle detection.\n\n3. **Topological Order** [1f]: When no cycles exist, provides a valid execution sequence where all dependencies come before their dependents.\n\n### Integration with Scoring\n\nThe analyzer returns `Insights` containing `NodeMetrics` for each task [1g]. These metrics feed into the overseer's recommendation scorer [1a], which combines them with mailbox message counts and recency to produce **weighted priority scores**. The graph metrics contribute 50% of the final score (30% critical path + 20% PageRank), ensuring that structurally important tasks rise to the top of recommendations.\n\n### Cycle Handling\n\nThe system gracefully handles **circular dependencies** using Tarjan's strongly connected components algorithm. When cycles exist, topological ordering is skipped, but PageRank and critical path scores are still computed (with cycle detection in the DFS preventing infinite loops)."
    },
    {
