        },
        {
          "id": "5e",
          "path": "skills/todo/main.go",
          "lineNumber": 496,
          "lineContent": "return skillout.Emit(rc, command, rec)",
          "title": "Emit recommendation output",
          "description": "Returns Recommendation struct with scored tasks to CLI"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "CLI Command Execution Flow\n├── agentctl todo recommend command \u003c-- 5a\n│   └── RunE handler \u003c-- cmd/agentctl/cmd/todo.go:253\n│       └── runTodoSkill(cmd, payload) \u003c-- 5b\n│           └── Execute todo skill binary\n│               └── Todo skill main() \u003c-- skills/todo/main.go:72\n│                   ├── Parse operation payload \u003c-- skills/todo/main.go:79\n│                   ├── Open task store \u003c-- skills/todo/main.go:88\n│                   ├── Open board store \u003c-- skills/todo/main.go:485\n│                   └── Handle recommend operation \u003c-- skills/todo/main.go:477\n│                       ├── NewScorer(store, board) \u003c-- 5c\n│                       ├── scorer.Recommend() \u003c-- 5d\n│                       │   └── [See Trace 2]\n│                       └── skillout.Emit(rec) \u003c-- 5e\n│                           └── Return JSON to CLI\n└── CLI displays recommendations",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nWhen managing complex projects with many interdependent tasks, users need to know **which task to work on next**. A naive approach (e.g., oldest first, or manual selection) fails to account for:\n\n1. **Dependency blocking**: Some tasks block many others (high critical path score)\n2. **Importance signals**: Admin or overseer messages indicate urgent attention needed\n3. **Graph centrality**: Tasks with many dependents are more important (PageRank)\n4. **Staleness**: Recently updated tasks may need less immediate attention\n\nThe **recommendation system** solves this by computing a weighted priority score that combines graph topology analysis with communication signals, then surfaces the highest-priority tasks to the user.\n\n## Details\n\n### Command Entry Point\n\nThe user invokes `agentctl todo recommend` [5a], which triggers a Cobra command handler [5b] that delegates to the `todo` skill binary with a JSON payload specifying the `recommend` operation.\n\n### Skill Execution\n\nThe todo skill's main function [5c] opens two storage backends:\n- **Task store**: SQLite database containing tasks and their dependencies\n- **Board store**: SQLite database containing mailbox messages [5c]\n\nIt then instantiates an `overseer.Scorer` [5c] and calls `Recommend()` [5d].\n\n### Scoring Algorithm\n\nThe scorer performs these steps:\n\n1. **Graph analysis**: Invokes `tasksgraph.Analyzer` to compute PageRank, critical path scores, and topological ordering from the task dependency graph\n2. **Message counting**: Queries the board store for unread messages per task, categorized by sender (admin vs. overseer)\n3. **Normalization**: Scales all metrics to 0-1 range to prevent any single factor from dominating\n4. **Weighted scoring**: Applies the formula [2e]:\n   - 30% critical path (longest dependency chain)\n   - 20% PageRank (graph centrality)\n   - 25% admin messages (human attention signals)\n   - 15% overseer messages (system coordination signals)\n   - 10% recency (inverse of days since last update)\n5. **Sorting**: Orders tasks by final score descending\n\n### Output\n\nThe skill emits a `Recommendation` struct [5e] containing:\n- **TopRecommended**: The highest-scored task\n- **Tasks**: Array of scored tasks with breakdown showing contribution of each factor\n- **TotalPending**: Count of incomplete tasks\n- **HasCycles**: Whether dependency cycles were detected\n\nThe CLI receives this JSON and displays it to the user, who can then act on the top recommendation with confidence that it accounts for both structural importance and communication urgency."
    },
    {
      "id": "6",
      "title": "Critical Path Calculation via Memoized DFS",
      "description": "Graph algorithm in internal/analysis/tasksgraph - computes longest path from each node to sinks using depth-first search",
      "locations": [
        {
          "id": "6a",
          "path": "internal/analysis/tasksgraph/graph.go",
          "lineNumber": 117,
          "lineContent": "criticalPaths := computeCriticalPaths(g, nodeToID)",
          "title": "Invoke critical path computation",
          "description": "Analyzer calls helper to compute critical path scores for all nodes"
        },
        {
          "id": "6b",
          "path": "internal/analysis/tasksgraph/graph.go",
          "lineNumber": 165,
          "lineContent": "var dfs func(n int64) int",
          "title": "Define DFS closure",
          "description": "Recursive function to compute longest path with memoization"
        },
        {
          "id": "6c",
          "path": "internal/analysis/tasksgraph/graph.go",
          "lineNumber": 172,
          "lineContent": "if visiting[n] { return 0 }",
          "title": "Detect cycles",
          "description": "Returns 0 if node is already in DFS stack (cycle detected)"
        },
        {
          "id": "6d",
          "path": "internal/analysis/tasksgraph/graph.go",
          "lineNumber": 179,
          "lineContent": "successors := g.From(n)",
          "title": "Get dependency successors",
          "description": "Retrieves nodes this task depends on (outgoing edges)"
        },
        {
          "id": "6e",
          "path": "internal/analysis/tasksgraph/graph.go",
          "lineNumber": 189,
          "lineContent": "pathLen := dfs(succ.ID()) + 1",
          "title": "Recurse and increment path",
          "description": "Recursively computes path length through each dependency"
        },
        {
          "id": "6f",
          "path": "internal/analysis/tasksgraph/graph.go",
          "lineNumber": 195,
          "lineContent": "memo[n] = maxPath",
          "title": "Memoize result",
          "description": "Caches longest path for this node to avoid recomputation"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Critical Path Calculation Flow\n├── Analyzer.Analyze() invokes computation \u003c-- 6a\n│   └── computeCriticalPaths(g, nodeToID) \u003c-- internal/analysis/tasksgraph/graph.go:160\n│       ├── Initialize memo \u0026 visiting maps \u003c-- internal/analysis/tasksgraph/graph.go:161\n│       ├── Define recursive DFS closure \u003c-- 6b\n│       │   ├── Check memo cache (return if found) \u003c-- internal/analysis/tasksgraph/graph.go:167\n│       │   ├── Cycle detection check \u003c-- 6c\n│       │   ├── Get outgoing edges (dependencies) \u003c-- 6d\n│       │   ├── If sink node (no deps): return 0 \u003c-- internal/analysis/tasksgraph/graph.go:180\n│       │   ├── For each successor: \u003c-- internal/analysis/tasksgraph/graph.go:187\n│       │   │   └── Recurse: dfs(succ) + 1 \u003c-- 6e\n│       │   ├── Take max of all paths \u003c-- internal/analysis/tasksgraph/graph.go:190\n│       │   └── Store in memo \u003c-- 6f\n│       └── Call dfs() for each node \u003c-- internal/analysis/tasksgraph/graph.go:199\n│           └── Build result map[taskID]int \u003c-- internal/analysis/tasksgraph/graph.go:203\n└── Return critical path scores \u003c-- internal/analysis/tasksgraph/graph.go:206",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nTask management systems need to answer: **\"What should I work on next?\"** When you have dozens of tasks with complex dependencies, this becomes non-trivial. A task blocking many others is more urgent than an isolated task. A task with admin attention is more important than one without feedback.\n\nThe **critical path score** solves this by computing the **longest dependency chain** from each task to completion [6a]. A task with a critical path score of 5 means there are 5 dependent tasks waiting on it—making it a high-priority blocker. This metric, combined with PageRank and message counts, feeds into the overseer's weighted recommendation system [2e].\n\n## Details\n\nThe algorithm uses **memoized depth-first search** to compute critical paths efficiently [6b]. Starting from any task node, it recursively explores all dependencies (outgoing edges in the graph) [6d]. \n\n**Sink nodes** (tasks with no dependencies) have a critical path score of 0 [6f]. For other tasks, the score is `1 + max(critical_path_of_dependencies)` [6e]. This means a task's score reflects the longest chain of work beneath it.\n\n**Cycle detection** is crucial [6c]—if a task depends on itself transitively, the algorithm returns 0 to avoid infinite recursion. The `visiting` map tracks nodes currently in the DFS stack to detect these cycles.\n\n**Memoization** prevents redundant computation [6f]. Once a node's critical path is calculated, it's cached in the `memo` map. This is essential because tasks can have multiple dependents, and without caching, the algorithm would recalculate the same paths exponentially.\n\nThe final result is a map from task ID to critical path score, which the overseer normalizes to 0-1 and weights at 30% in the final recommendation score [2d]."
    },
